    "slug": "remote-therapist-work-from-home",
    "title": "Can a Licensed Therapist Work Fully Remote From Home?",
    "primary_question": "Can a licensed therapist work fully remote from home?",
    "summary": "Yes. Many therapists work fully remote by telehealth. What you need: licensure where clients are, a private HIPAA-ready setup, and a plan for isolation.",
    "body_markdown": "## Can therapists work fully remote?\n\nYes. Telehealth is now an ordinary way to deliver outpatient therapy, and many therapists never see clients in person. Remote work is common in private practice, contractor networks, telehealth platforms, and some employers, including VA.\n\n## What do you need to work from home?\n\n- **A license in each client's state.** Your clients must be located in states where you're licensed, or where you hold a compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n- **A private space.** A room with a door where no one can overhear, including family and smart speakers.\n- **A HIPAA-appropriate platform** from a vendor that signs a business associate agreement. See [HIPAA Telehealth Setup](/resources/for-therapists/hipaa-telehealth-setup).\n- **Reliable internet** and a backup plan, like a phone hotspot.\n- **Secure devices:** passwords, updates, encryption, and no shared family computer.\n- **An emergency plan** for each client: their location, an emergency contact, and local resources.\n- **Malpractice coverage** that includes telehealth.\n\n## What's hard about remote work?\n\n- **Isolation.** No hallway consults or lunch with colleagues.\n- **Boundaries.** Work can spread into the whole house and day.\n- **Screen fatigue.** Back-to-back video sessions tire people differently.\n- **Emergencies at a distance.** You can't walk a client to the ER.\n\nPlan for each: a peer consultation group, set work hours and a set workspace, breaks between sessions, and a crisis protocol. VA's free [PTSD Consultation Program](https://www.ptsd.va.gov/professional/consult/index.asp) is one source of consultation for therapists treating Veterans.\n\n## Is remote therapy as effective?\n\nFor many outpatient clients and conditions, telehealth works well, and VA uses it widely for mental health care. It isn't right for everyone; some clients need in-person or higher levels of care. Screen for fit.\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first, and its therapists work remotely. ValorWell provides the EHR, scheduling, and telehealth tools. You set your availability, choose your capacity, and see Veterans, spouses, and children of Veterans located in the states where you're licensed. ValorWell needs therapists in every state.\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Your licenses decide your reach.** Remote doesn't mean you can see anyone anywhere.\n- **ValorWell isn't a crisis service.** Clients in crisis need the Veterans Crisis Line (dial 988, then press 1) or emergency services.\n- **You supply your workspace** and a reliable connection.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "You need a private space where sessions can't be overheard. It doesn't have to be a separate building.",
        "question": "Do I need a separate office to do telehealth from home?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Your location matters less than the client's, but check your own state's rules and your malpractice policy.",
        "question": "Can I do telehealth while traveling?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "No license requires it in most states, but telehealth training helps with safety planning and engagement.",
        "question": "Do remote therapists need special training?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes. What matters is that you're licensed where your clients are located.",
        "question": "Can I work remotely for a company in another state?"
      }
    ],
