    "slug": "veteran-eligibility",
    "title": "Can a Veteran Get CHAMPVA? Veterans and Parents Explained",
    "primary_question": "Can a Veteran get CHAMPVA coverage, and can a Veteran's parent qualify?",
    "summary": "Veterans usually get care through VA health care, not CHAMPVA. Here are the exceptions, and why a Veteran's parent rarely qualifies.",
    "body_markdown": "## Can a Veteran use CHAMPVA for their own care?\n\nNot based on their own service. CHAMPVA covers the spouse, children, and survivors of qualifying Veterans, under [38 U.S.C. 1781](https://uscode.house.gov/view.xhtml?req=%28title%3A38+section%3A1781+edition%3Aprelim%29) and [38 CFR 17.271](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-38/chapter-I/part-17/section-17.271). It isn't a health plan for the Veteran.\n\nA Veteran's own care runs through [VA health care](https://www.va.gov/health-care/eligibility/), and when VA can't provide the care in time or close enough, through [VA Community Care](/resources/va-community-care/who-qualifies).\n\n## When can a Veteran also get CHAMPVA?\n\nThere are two real exceptions.\n\n### 1. The Veteran is married to another qualifying Veteran\n\nIf both spouses are Veterans, each may qualify for CHAMPVA as the spouse or surviving spouse of the other, as long as the other Veteran meets a CHAMPVA category. VA's [CHAMPVA page](https://www.va.gov/family-and-caregiver-benefits/health-and-disability/champva/) says it directly: if you and your spouse are both Veterans, you may each qualify for both VA health care and CHAMPVA.\n\nExample: Maria and James both served. James is rated permanently and totally disabled. Maria can qualify for CHAMPVA as James's spouse, and she can still use VA health care based on her own service.\n\nThe usual rules still apply. Neither spouse can be eligible for TRICARE. So if Maria is a military retiree with TRICARE, she can't use CHAMPVA. More here: [CHAMPVA for spouses of P&T Veterans](/resources/champva/spouse-permanent-total-eligibility).\n\n### 2. The Veteran is approved as a Primary Family Caregiver\n\nVA's caregiver program, the Program of Comprehensive Assistance for Family Caregivers (PCAFC), can give CHAMPVA to the designated Primary Family Caregiver. A caregiver who happens to be a Veteran isn't automatically excluded. But this route has a strict rule: the caregiver can't be covered by another health plan, such as TRICARE, Medicare, Medicaid, or job-based insurance. More here: [CHAMPVA for Primary Family Caregivers](/resources/champva/primary-family-caregiver-eligibility).\n\n## Can a Veteran's parent get CHAMPVA?\n\nOnly through the caregiver route. There's no CHAMPVA category for a Veteran's dependent parent, even if the Veteran supports them or VA counts them as a dependent for compensation.\n\nA parent who is approved as the Veteran's Primary Family Caregiver under PCAFC, and who has no other health coverage, can get CHAMPVA that way. A parent on Medicare can't, because Medicare counts as other coverage under the caregiver rule.\n\n## Quick check\n\n| Situation | CHAMPVA? |\n| --- | --- |\n| Veteran, based on their own service or rating | No. Use VA health care. |\n| Veteran married to a P&T Veteran, no TRICARE | Possibly, as a spouse |\n| Veteran who is the surviving spouse of a qualifying Veteran, no TRICARE | Possibly, as a survivor |\n| Veteran approved as Primary Family Caregiver, no other coverage | Possibly, as a caregiver |\n| Veteran's parent | Only as a Primary Family Caregiver with no other coverage |\n\nIf one of these fits, apply and let VA decide. More here: [Who qualifies for CHAMPVA?](/resources/champva/who-qualifies)",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "No, you get VA health care. Your spouse and children may qualify for CHAMPVA because of your rating.",
        "question": "I'm a 100% P&T Veteran. Do I get CHAMPVA?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Possibly. Each of you can qualify as the other's spouse if the other Veteran meets a CHAMPVA category and neither is TRICARE-eligible.",
        "question": "Can my spouse and I both use CHAMPVA if we're both Veterans?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "A Veteran who qualifies for CHAMPVA as a spouse can have both. VA health care covers care tied to their own eligibility; CHAMPVA covers them as a family member.",
        "question": "Can a Veteran use VA health care and CHAMPVA at the same time?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Not as a dependent. The only route is PCAFC Primary Family Caregiver status with no other health coverage.",
        "question": "My parent lives with me and I support them. Can they get CHAMPVA?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Usually not, because military retirees are eligible for TRICARE, and TRICARE eligibility rules out CHAMPVA.",
        "question": "Does a military retiree Veteran qualify for CHAMPVA through a spouse?"
      }
    ],
    "audience_tags": [
      "beneficiary"
    ],
