    "slug": "which-licenses-can-serve-veterans",
    "title": "Can LPCs, LMFTs, and LCSWs Treat CHAMPVA and VA Clients?",
    "primary_question": "Can LPCs, LMFTs, LCSWs, and psychologists treat CHAMPVA and VA Community Care clients?",
    "summary": "Yes. CHAMPVA pays any state-licensed provider working within scope, and VA Community Care networks include counselors, MFTs, social workers, and psychologists.",
    "body_markdown": "## Which licenses can treat CHAMPVA clients?\n\nAny license that lets you provide the service in your state, used within its scope. [38 CFR 17.270](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-38/chapter-I/part-17/section-17.270) defines an authorized provider as one licensed or certified by the state to provide the service. The CHAMPVA Guidebook adds that the provider must work within the scope of that license.\n\nFor outpatient psychotherapy, that generally includes:\n\n- Licensed professional counselors, under whatever title your state uses (LPC, LPCC, LMHC, LCPC, and others)\n- Licensed marriage and family therapists (LMFT)\n- Licensed clinical social workers (LCSW, or your state's independent clinical title)\n- Licensed psychologists\n\nCHAMPVA has no network, so there's no separate CHAMPVA credentialing. See [Authorized CHAMPVA Providers](/resources/champva/authorized-providers).\n\n## What about VA Community Care?\n\nVA Community Care pays community providers who are in the [Community Care Network](https://department.va.gov/vha/community-care/partnerships/community-care-network/) (CCN) or who have a Veterans Care Agreement with a VA facility. Behavioral health networks include counselors, marriage and family therapists, social workers, and psychologists. The regional administrator (Optum or TriWest) credentials each provider, and VA's rules on eligible providers are in [38 CFR 17.4030](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-38/chapter-I/part-17/section-17.4030).\n\nVA also checks that providers aren't excluded from federal health programs.\n\n## Can pre-licensed clinicians bill these programs?\n\nGenerally not under their own name. An associate-level license (such as LPC Associate, LMFT Associate, or LMSW) requires supervision. Whether supervised services can be billed, and how, depends on the program and state law, and it's an area where mistakes lead to recoupments. Check with the program before billing anything a supervisee provides.\n\n## Does the license have to match the client's state?\n\nYes. For telehealth, you must be licensed where the client is located. That rule comes from state licensing law, not from VA, and it applies to both programs. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## What about TRICARE and Medicare?\n\nThey have their own provider rules. TRICARE has specific certification requirements for mental health counselors. Medicare began paying mental health counselors and marriage and family therapists in 2024. See [How Therapists Become TRICARE Providers](/resources/for-therapists/become-tricare-provider-therapist) and [Medicare for LPCs and LMFTs](/resources/for-therapists/medicare-mental-health-counselors-mfts).\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nValorWell works with any independently licensed mental health therapist whose license can bill insurance: LPC, LPCC, LMHC and other state counselor titles, LMFT, LCSW, and psychologists. ValorWell handles the credentialing for its pathways; you provide your license and professional information.\n\nRight now, ValorWell isn't working with associates who need supervision or with prescribers. See [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Scope still applies.** A license that allows therapy doesn't allow psychological testing or prescribing unless your state says so.\n- **Program rules can change.** Check VA's current provider guidance before relying on any list.\n- **Licensure isn't credentialing.** For VA Community Care, you still need to be credentialed in the network or hold a VCA before you see Veterans.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Yes, if the LPC is independently licensed in the state and working within scope. CHAMPVA doesn't require a network contract.",
        "question": "Can an LPC bill CHAMPVA?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "No. You bill with your NPI. CHAMPVA doesn't credential providers the way insurance networks do.",
        "question": "Do I need a separate CHAMPVA provider number?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Not as an independent provider. VA Community Care uses independently licensed clinicians credentialed by the regional administrator.",
        "question": "Can an LMSW treat VA Community Care clients?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes, within their license. Under VA Community Care, testing needs to be on the authorization or approved through a Request for Service.",
        "question": "Can a psychologist provide testing through these programs?"
      }
    ],
