    "slug": "working-for-multiple-therapy-platforms",
    "title": "Can Therapists Work for More Than One Practice or Platform?",
    "primary_question": "Can therapists work for more than one telehealth platform or practice at once?",
    "summary": "Yes, if your contracts are nonexclusive. What to check for exclusivity and non-compete clauses, and how to manage scheduling and records across jobs.",
    "body_markdown": "## Can you work for more than one practice or platform?\n\nUsually yes, as a contractor. Independent contractors typically work for more than one client; that's part of what makes them independent. Many therapists combine a private practice, a contract role, and a part-time job.\n\nW-2 jobs are different. Some employers restrict outside work, especially work that competes with them.\n\n## What should you check in each contract?\n\n- **Exclusivity:** can you work with other organizations at all?\n- **Non-compete:** are there limits on similar work during or after the contract? See [Are Non-Compete Clauses Enforceable?](/resources/for-therapists/non-compete-therapist-contracts)\n- **Non-solicitation:** limits on contacting the organization's clients or staff.\n- **Conflicts of interest:** rules about seeing the same client in two places.\n- **Records:** who owns records created in each system.\n\n## How do you manage multiple roles well?\n\n- **One calendar.** Track every session across every system in one place to avoid double-booking.\n- **Separate records.** Document each client in the system that owns their care.\n- **Clear boundaries.** Don't move clients between organizations without checking your contracts and the client's choice.\n- **Malpractice for all settings.** Confirm your policy covers each one.\n- **Capacity.** Multiple roles make it easy to overcommit. Set a total weekly limit.\n- **Taxes.** Multiple 1099s mean multiple income streams to track.\n\n## What are the benefits?\n\nDiversified income, so one slow referral source doesn't sink your month. Variety in your caseload. And a way to try a new setting without leaving the one you have.\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nValorWell's arrangement is nonexclusive. ValorWell therapists can keep a private practice, other contracts, or other employment. You set your ValorWell availability in its EHR around everything else, and can turn new clients on or off whenever your other work changes.\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Your other contracts still matter.** Check them for exclusivity or non-compete terms before adding work.\n- **Licensure follows the client** in every role.\n- **Your own malpractice coverage** should cover your ValorWell work along with everything else.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Generally avoid it. It complicates records, billing, and contracts. Choose one setting per client.",
        "question": "Can I see the same client through two organizations?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Check your contract. Some require disclosure of outside work, especially in W-2 roles.",
        "question": "Do I have to tell one employer about my other work?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Contracts can include exclusivity, but strong control like that can raise worker classification questions.",
        "question": "Can a contractor be required to work exclusively for one company?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "It depends on your systems and capacity. Many therapists find two or three is the practical limit.",
        "question": "How many contract roles can I realistically juggle?"
      }
    ],
