    "slug": "insurance-billing-burden-therapists",
    "title": "How Therapists Can Spend Less Time on Insurance Billing",
    "primary_question": "How can therapists spend less time on insurance billing and paperwork?",
    "summary": "Where therapists' insurance billing time goes, from eligibility to denials, and the main ways to cut it: tools, billing services, groups, or networks.",
    "body_markdown": "## Where does insurance billing time go?\n\nFor most therapists in private practice, it's spread across a dozen small jobs that add up:\n\n- Checking eligibility and benefits before first sessions.\n- Getting and tracking authorizations.\n- Coding and submitting claims.\n- Posting payments and reconciling what came in.\n- Fixing rejected claims and appealing denials.\n- Calling payers about missing payments.\n- Credentialing and recredentialing.\n- Collecting copays and cost shares from clients.\n\nGovernment programs add their own layers. CHAMPVA has separate payer IDs for eligibility and claims and pays only by EFT. VA Community Care runs on referrals, authorizations, and Requests for Service.\n\n## How can you cut it down?\n\nFrom least to most hands-off:\n\n1. **Tighten your process.** Verify eligibility before every first session, collect other-insurance information at intake, and check claim status before resubmitting. Clean front-end work prevents most back-end work.\n2. **Use better tools.** A practice management system with eligibility checks, claim scrubbing, and electronic remittance saves hours.\n3. **Hire a billing service.** You keep the practice; they handle claims and follow-up for a fee.\n4. **Change settings.** In a group practice or a supported network, billing is someone else's job entirely.\n\nSee [Billing Service vs. Doing Your Own Billing](/resources/for-therapists/billing-service-vs-own-billing).\n\n## How do you decide?\n\nTrack your billing time for a month. Then ask:\n\n- What is that time worth to you, in sessions or in life outside work?\n- Which payers cause most of the trouble?\n- Do you enjoy running the business side, or tolerate it?\n\nThere's no wrong answer. Some therapists like owning every part of their practice. Others would rather never see a remittance again.\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nValorWell handles the billing side completely for its pathways: credentialing administration, eligibility, authorizations, claims submission, payer follow-up, reimbursement administration, and denials. Your part is the session and a complete note.\n\nYou're paid weekly for sessions you documented that pay period, and denials don't come out of your pay. The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep billing your own private practice clients separately if you want.\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Accurate documentation is still yours.** Clean claims start with complete notes.\n- **Handing off billing means handing off some control,** including over rates and payer mix.\n- **Billing services vary in quality.** Ask about denial rates, reporting, and who owns your data.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "It varies by caseload and payer mix. Tracking your own time for a month is the best way to know.",
        "question": "How much time do therapists spend on billing?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Verifying eligibility and other insurance before the first session. It prevents many denials.",
        "question": "What's the most time-saving billing habit?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "It depends on your market. Insurance brings clients who can't pay privately, but adds admin. Many therapists take a few payers rather than many.",
        "question": "Is it worth taking insurance at all?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes. Some therapists keep commercial billing and work with a network for VA programs, or the reverse.",
        "question": "Can I hand off billing for just one program?"
      }
    ],
