    "slug": "insurance-credentialing-for-therapists",
    "title": "Insurance Credentialing for Therapists: Steps and Timelines",
    "primary_question": "What does insurance credentialing involve for therapists, and how long does it take?",
    "summary": "What insurance credentialing involves for therapists: documents, applications, CAQH, timelines, recredentialing, and who can do the work for you.",
    "body_markdown": "## What is insurance credentialing?\n\nIt's how a payer verifies that you're qualified before it lets you bill as an in-network provider. The payer checks your license, education, work history, malpractice coverage, and whether you've been disciplined or excluded from federal programs. Contracting, agreeing to the payer's rates and rules, usually happens alongside it.\n\n## What do you need?\n\n- **An individual NPI** from [NPPES](https://nppes.cms.hhs.gov/). See [How to Get an NPI and CAQH Profile](/resources/for-therapists/npi-caqh-setup-therapists).\n- **An active, independent license** in each state where you'll see clients.\n- **Malpractice insurance** at the limits the payer requires.\n- **A CAQH ProView profile** for payers that use it.\n- **Practice details:** tax ID, practice address (or telehealth setup), and W-9.\n- **Education and work history,** often with explanations for gaps.\n\n## What are the steps?\n\n1. Decide which payers you want.\n2. Check whether each payer's network is open for your specialty and area.\n3. Submit the application and contract request.\n4. Respond quickly to requests for more information.\n5. Receive your contract and effective date.\n6. Confirm your provider record is loaded correctly before billing.\n\n## How long does credentialing take?\n\nIt varies by payer and season, often weeks to a few months. Delays usually come from missing documents, slow responses, or closed panels. Don't schedule clients under a payer until you have an effective date.\n\n## What about VA programs?\n\n- **CHAMPVA** has no network, so no credentialing. You bill as a licensed provider.\n- **VA Community Care** requires credentialing with Optum or TriWest for the Community Care Network, or through VA's verification process for a Veterans Care Agreement. See [How Therapists Join the VA Community Care Network](/resources/for-therapists/join-va-community-care-network-therapist).\n- **Medicare** requires enrollment through PECOS for counselors and MFTs.\n\n## Is credentialing one and done?\n\nNo. Payers recredential every few years, and you'll need to keep CAQH and licenses current, report changes, and re-attest.\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nValorWell manages credentialing for its care pathways. You supply your professional information and documents; ValorWell handles the processing, status tracking, follow-up, and communication with credentialing entities.\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Credentialing doesn't guarantee immediate approval or activation.** Payers set their own timelines.\n- **Your credentials are still yours to maintain:** an active license, current malpractice, and accurate information.\n- **You can't see clients through a payer** until credentialing for that payer is complete.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Not as in-network with that payer. You can see them as private pay, or wait for your effective date.",
        "question": "Can I see clients while credentialing is pending?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "A payer isn't adding providers of your type in your area. You can ask to be waitlisted or appeal with a case for need.",
        "question": "What is a closed panel?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "No, but many commercial payers use it. Government programs like CHAMPVA don't.",
        "question": "Do I need CAQH for every insurer?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes. Credentialing services, group practices, and networks often handle it.",
        "question": "Can someone credential for me?"
      }
    ],
