    "slug": "counselor-license-titles-by-state",
    "title": "LPC, LPCC, LMHC, LCPC: Why Counselor License Titles Differ",
    "primary_question": "LPC, LPCC, LMHC, LCPC: why do counselor license titles differ by state, and do they matter for insurance?",
    "summary": "Counselor license titles vary by state: LPC, LPCC, LMHC, LCPC, LCMHC, and more. What they mean, independent vs. associate levels, and why payers care.",
    "body_markdown": "## Why are there so many counselor license titles?\n\nBecause each state licenses counselors under its own law, and states chose different names. They describe the same profession: master's-level clinical mental health counselors.\n\nSome common titles:\n\n| Title | Stands for | Examples of states using it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| LPC | Licensed Professional Counselor | Many states, including Texas and Virginia |\n| LPCC | Licensed Professional Clinical Counselor | California, Ohio |\n| LMHC | Licensed Mental Health Counselor | Florida, New York, Massachusetts, Washington |\n| LCPC | Licensed Clinical Professional Counselor | Illinois, Maryland, Maine |\n| LCMHC | Licensed Clinical Mental Health Counselor | North Carolina, New Hampshire, Vermont |\n\nTitles change. North Carolina, for example, renamed its LPC license to LCMHC. Your state board is the authority.\n\n## Independent vs. associate: why does the level matter?\n\nMost states have two or more levels:\n\n- **An associate or provisional level** for counselors finishing supervised hours, often with titles like LPC Associate, LPCA, or LMHC-Intern.\n- **An independent level** that allows practice without supervision.\n\nIn some states, a title that's independent elsewhere is the supervised level. In Illinois, for example, the LPC is the earlier license and the LCPC is the independent one. In Ohio, the LPC is the earlier license and the LPCC is independent.\n\nPayers care about the level. Most insurers and government programs pay independently licensed counselors. Billing for supervised services follows separate, payer-specific rules.\n\n## Does the title matter for VA programs and Medicare?\n\n- **CHAMPVA** pays providers licensed by the state for the service, working within scope, under [38 CFR 17.270](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-38/chapter-I/part-17/section-17.270). The title doesn't matter; the license authority does.\n- **Medicare** groups these titles as \"mental health counselors\" and requires state licensure plus post-degree supervised experience. See [CMS's page](https://www.cms.gov/medicare/payment/fee-schedules/physician-fee-schedule/marriage-family-therapists-mental-health-counselors).\n- **TRICARE** has its own certification requirements for counselors.\n\n## What about licensure in other states?\n\nYour title may change when you get licensed in another state, even though your profession didn't. The Counseling Compact is creating a privilege to practice across member states. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact).\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nValorWell accepts any independent counselor license that can bill insurance, under whatever title your state uses: LPC, LPCC, LMHC, LCPC, LCMHC, and others. It also works with LMFTs, LCSWs, and psychologists. Associate-level and supervised licenses aren't accepted right now.\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Check your board.** Titles and levels change, and this page gives examples, not a complete list.\n- **Scope varies by state.** Some states differ on diagnosis or assessment authority for counselors.\n- **Payer rules vary.** Confirm a payer recognizes your license level before billing.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "They're the same profession under different state titles. Both are generally independent clinical counselor licenses.",
        "question": "Is an LMHC the same as an LPC?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "In states where LPCC is the independent license, generally yes, subject to each payer's credentialing.",
        "question": "Can an LPCC bill insurance?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "In some states, like Illinois, the LPC is the earlier license and the LCPC is the independent one. Elsewhere, LPC is the independent title.",
        "question": "What's the difference between an LPC and an LCPC?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "It can. You'll hold that state's title for the license it issues.",
        "question": "Does my title change if I get licensed in another state?"
      }
    ],
    "audience_tags": [
      "provider"
    ],
