    "slug": "mst-what-community-therapists-should-know",
    "title": "Military Sexual Trauma: What Community Therapists Should Know",
    "primary_question": "What should community therapists know about military sexual trauma and VA's free MST care?",
    "summary": "VA provides free care for military sexual trauma with no proof required, even for many with other-than-honorable discharges. What therapists should know.",
    "body_markdown": "## What is military sexual trauma?\n\nVA defines military sexual trauma (MST) as sexual assault or threatening sexual harassment experienced during military service. It's an experience, not a diagnosis. It can affect people of any gender, branch, or era. See [VA's MST page](https://www.va.gov/health-care/health-needs-conditions/military-sexual-trauma/).\n\nIf a client is in crisis, the Veterans Crisis Line is available any time: dial 988, then press 1, or text 838255.\n\n## What free care does VA offer for MST?\n\nVA provides free treatment for any physical or mental health condition related to MST. Key points for therapists to know:\n\n- **No proof needed.** The person doesn't have to have reported it at the time or have other evidence.\n- **No disability rating needed.** Service connection isn't required.\n- **Broad eligibility.** Most former service members qualify, including many with an other-than-honorable discharge or less than two years of service, even if they can't get other VA care.\n- **An MST Coordinator at every VA medical center** can help a client get connected.\n- **Vet Centers** also offer counseling for MST.\n\n## Why does this matter if you're treating the client?\n\nA client who's working with you can still use VA's MST services. Some want an additional specialized program or group. Some need a VA connection for the medical side of MST-related conditions. Some don't want anything to do with VA, and that's their call.\n\nKnowing what's free gives your client options. It doesn't have to change your treatment.\n\n## What should therapists keep in mind?\n\n- **Disclosure is often delayed.** Many survivors don't tell anyone for years. A client may disclose MST long after you've started working together.\n- **Betrayal is often part of it.** The perpetrator may have been a fellow service member or someone in the chain of command, someone the client was supposed to trust with their life.\n- **Ask with care.** VA screens every Veteran it sees for MST. In private practice, a gentle, direct question during intake normalizes it.\n- **Let the client lead.** Don't push for details the client isn't ready to share.\n\n## Where can therapists learn more?\n\nVA offers a free course for community providers, *Military Sexual Trauma for Community Mental Health Providers*, with CE credit through VHA TRAIN. See [VA's community care training page](https://department.va.gov/vha/community-care/training-and-resources/) and VA's [MST resources for providers](https://mentalhealth.va.gov/healthcare-providers/mst.asp).\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nValorWell therapists decide which referrals fit their training. If MST work is outside your experience, you can decline those referrals without pressure. If it's your specialty, that's welcome. Treatment approach and pacing stay with you.\n\nFor Veterans, MST-related therapy may come through VA Community Care when VA authorizes it. See [VA Community Care for MST](/resources/va-community-care/military-sexual-trauma-care).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **VA decides eligibility** for its MST services. You can point clients there, but you can't confirm what they'll receive.\n- **Disability compensation is separate.** Free MST-related care doesn't depend on a disability claim, and a claim has its own evidence rules.\n- **This is orientation, not training.** Trauma work with MST survivors calls for trauma-focused training and consultation.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "No. VA doesn't require a report at the time or other proof to provide free MST-related care.",
        "question": "Does a Veteran need to have reported MST to get VA care for it?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "In many cases, yes. VA's MST services are open to most former service members, including many with other-than-honorable discharges.",
        "question": "Can someone with an other-than-honorable discharge get MST care from VA?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "No. MST happens to people of every gender. Men are often less likely to disclose it.",
        "question": "Is MST only something that happens to women?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Every VA medical center has one. Call the nearest VA medical center and ask for the MST Coordinator.",
        "question": "How do I find a client's MST Coordinator?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes. VHA TRAIN offers a free MST course for community mental health providers with CE credit.",
        "question": "Is there free MST training for community therapists?"
      }
    ],
