    "primary_question": "Why do therapy employers chase clinicians about notes, and is there a better way?",
    "summary": "Why therapy employers chase clinicians about notes, what documentation standards actually require, and a model that keeps accountability without nagging.",
    "body_markdown": "## Why do employers chase therapists about notes?\n\nBecause an unfinished note is a problem for everyone downstream. Most payers expect a session to be documented before it's billed. Late notes delay revenue, weaken the record if a claim is audited, and can leave another clinician without the information they need in a crisis.\n\nSo the concern is legitimate. The usual fixes are the problem: daily reminder emails, dashboards of overdue notes, write-ups, and holding pay until every note is done.\n\n## What do documentation standards actually require?\n\nThere's no single national note deadline for therapists. Expectations come from several places:\n\n- **Payers**, which generally require documentation supporting each billed service.\n- **Licensing boards and ethics codes**, which require accurate, timely records. Some state rules set specific timeframes.\n- **Your employer or contract**, which may set its own deadline.\n- **Good clinical practice.** Notes written soon after a session are more accurate.\n\nWhatever the system, timely documentation matters. The question is how an organization encourages it.\n\n## Why doesn't nagging work well?\n\nIt treats licensed professionals like they need supervision on basic tasks, and it puts managers in the role of hall monitor. Clinicians who feel policed tend to resent it, and the relationship becomes about compliance rather than support. It also doesn't fix the root causes of late notes: clunky EHRs, overbooked schedules, and no protected time.\n\n## What works better?\n\n- **Make notes easier.** Better templates and tools that fit how therapists work.\n- **Protect time.** Schedules that leave room for documentation.\n- **Align incentives.** Tie pay to completed documentation, so clinicians decide when to write and the consequence is natural, not punitive.\n- **Fix systems first.** When notes keep running late, look at the tools and workload before blaming the clinician.\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nValorWell doesn't have an official note deadline, and nobody chases therapists about notes. Therapists are paid weekly for the sessions they documented during that pay period. If you see a client this week and finish the note next week, that session is paid next week.\n\nThat puts accountability where it belongs, with the professional, and keeps the relationship positive. ValorWell doesn't have to go looking for problems or micromanage.\n\nThe EHR helps too. It was built with input from practicing clinicians and includes AI-assisted documentation. And if a documentation issue does come up, ValorWell's first step is to see what adjustment or support would make the work easier.\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Timeliness still matters.** Payer, ethics, and licensing standards for records still apply, and some states set their own timeframes.\n- **AI assistance doesn't replace you.** Clinicians review, edit, and own their notes.\n- **Pay follows documentation.** An undocumented session isn't paid until its note is completed.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "There's no single national deadline. Payers, state rules, and employers may set expectations, so check the ones that apply to you.",
        "question": "Is there a legal deadline for therapy notes?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "For W-2 employees, wage laws limit withholding earned wages. Contractors' payment terms depend on the contract.",
        "question": "Can an employer withhold pay for late notes?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "It varies by format and complexity. Good templates and tools can shorten it without losing what payers and clinicians need.",
        "question": "How long should a therapy progress note take?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Only if they're set up for it, including a business associate agreement with the vendor. Clinicians still review every note.",
        "question": "Do AI documentation tools meet HIPAA requirements?"
      }
    ],
