    "slug": "psychosis-mental-illness-presumption",
    "title": "Presumptive VA Care for Psychosis and Other Mental Illness",
    "primary_question": "Who qualifies for presumptive VA care for psychosis or other mental illness?",
    "summary": "Certain Veterans who developed psychosis, or Gulf War Veterans with other mental illness, within 2 years after service can get VA care for it with no copays.",
    "body_markdown": "## What is the psychosis and mental illness presumption?\n\nA rule that makes certain Veterans eligible for VA treatment of psychosis or other mental illness without proving it's service connected. It's in [38 CFR 17.109](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-38/chapter-I/part-17/section-17.109).\n\nThis is a health care rule. It covers treatment, not disability compensation. A disability rating is a separate claim.\n\n## Who qualifies for the psychosis presumption?\n\nVeterans of these periods who developed psychosis:\n\n- **within two years after leaving active service;** and\n- **before the cutoff date** for their period:\n\n| Period | Psychosis must have developed before |\n| --- | --- |\n| World War II | July 26, 1949 |\n| Korean conflict | February 1, 1957 |\n| Vietnam era | May 8, 1977 |\n| Persian Gulf War | The end of the two-year period starting on the last day of the Persian Gulf War |\n\nThe care covers treatment of an active psychosis.\n\n## Who qualifies for the other mental illness presumption?\n\nOnly Persian Gulf War Veterans. If you developed a mental illness other than psychosis, like depression or PTSD, within two years after leaving active service and before the end of the two-year period starting on the last day of the Persian Gulf War, you're presumed eligible for treatment of that active mental illness.\n\nThe Persian Gulf War hasn't officially ended. Its end date is set by law or presidential proclamation, so this window hasn't closed for Gulf War Veterans.\n\n## Does length of service matter?\n\nNo. Under 38 CFR 17.109(c), eligibility for this care, and the copay exemption, doesn't depend on how long you served on active duty.\n\n## Are there copays?\n\nNo. 38 CFR 17.109 exempts this care from VA copays for outpatient care, medications, and inpatient care, under 17.108, 17.110, and 17.111. [38 CFR 17.108](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-38/chapter-I/part-17/section-17.108) also lists these Veterans among those not subject to outpatient copays for this care.\n\n## How does this connect to Community Care?\n\nThis rule decides whether VA will treat the condition. Community Care decides where. If you qualify under 17.109 and VA can't provide mental health care within its [20-day and 30-minute standards](/resources/va-community-care/mental-health-access-standards), or Community Care is in your [best medical interest](/resources/va-community-care/best-medical-interest), VA can refer you to a community provider.\n\nRoutine Community Care usually requires enrollment in VA health care, so if you aren't enrolled, ask about enrolling. See [Who Is a Covered Veteran?](/resources/va-community-care/covered-veteran-definition)\n\n## How do I use this rule?\n\n1. **Gather your records.** Your DD-214 showing your service period and discharge date, and any medical records showing when symptoms started.\n2. **Contact VA's enrollment office** or call 877-222-8387 and say you may qualify for care under 38 CFR 17.109.\n3. **Ask VA to note the presumption** in your record so the copay exemption applies.\n4. **Ask for mental health care,** and if VA can't meet the access standards, ask about Community Care.\n\nTiming can be hard to show years later. Records from the first two years after discharge, like treatment notes or statements from people who knew you then, can help.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "No. It covers VA treatment only. A rating requires a separate disability claim.",
        "question": "Does this presumption give me a disability rating?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "For Gulf War Veterans, it covers mental illness other than psychosis that started within two years after service and within the qualifying period.",
        "question": "Does the mental illness presumption cover PTSD?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "No. 38 CFR 17.109 exempts it from VA outpatient, medication, and inpatient copays.",
        "question": "Do I pay copays for this care?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes, if you meet a Community Care eligibility rule and VA approves the referral.",
        "question": "Can this care be provided through Community Care?"
      }
    ],
