    "slug": "progress-notes-vs-psychotherapy-notes",
    "title": "Progress Notes vs. Psychotherapy Notes Under HIPAA",
    "primary_question": "What is the difference between progress notes and psychotherapy notes under HIPAA?",
    "summary": "HIPAA treats psychotherapy notes differently from progress notes. What each includes, who can access them, and what payers can ask for.",
    "body_markdown": "## What's the difference?\n\nUnder HIPAA, **psychotherapy notes** are notes a mental health professional records to document or analyze the contents of a counseling session, kept separate from the rest of the medical record ([45 CFR 164.501](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-C/part-164/subpart-E/section-164.501)). Everything else is part of the regular record, including what most therapists call progress notes.\n\n| | Progress notes | Psychotherapy notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Where kept | In the medical record | Separate from the record |\n| Contents | Diagnosis, symptoms, functional status, treatment plan, interventions, progress, session times, test results, medications | The therapist's analysis and impressions of session content |\n| Needed for billing | Yes | No |\n| Client's right of access | Yes | No, though you may choose to share |\n| Disclosure | Treatment, payment, and operations rules apply | Authorization generally required |\n\n## What can't go in psychotherapy notes?\n\nHHS lists what the definition excludes: medication prescribing and monitoring, session start and stop times, treatment modalities and frequencies, clinical test results, and summaries of diagnosis, functional status, treatment plan, symptoms, prognosis, and progress. See [HHS's explanation](https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/faq/2088/does-hipaa-provide-extra-protections-mental-health-information-compared-other-health.html).\n\nIn other words, anything a payer would need lives in the progress note.\n\n## Who can see psychotherapy notes?\n\n- **Disclosure generally requires the client's authorization,** even for treatment by another provider, under 45 CFR 164.508(a)(2). Exceptions include disclosures required by law, such as mandated reporting.\n- **Clients don't have a HIPAA right to access them,** per [HHS's guidance on access](https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/faq/what-personal-health-information-do-individuals/index.html). You can choose to share them.\n- **Payers can't require them** as a condition of payment. They can ask for progress notes and the record.\n\n## Do you need to keep psychotherapy notes?\n\nNo. Many therapists don't keep them at all. If you do, they only get special protection when kept separate from the record. Notes stored in the same field as your progress note are part of the record.\n\nState laws can add more protection or access rights. Check yours.\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nValorWell's EHR is built around the documentation therapists need for care and clean claims, with AI-assisted progress notes. Whether you also keep separate psychotherapy notes is a clinical decision for you, within HIPAA and your state's rules. ValorWell's billing uses the progress note, not psychotherapy notes.\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **State law can be stricter than HIPAA.** Some states give clients broader access or add protections.\n- **Records requests and subpoenas** have their own rules; get advice before releasing records you're unsure about.\n- **This isn't legal advice.** For specific situations, contact your licensing board or a health law attorney.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "A health plan can't require psychotherapy notes as a condition of payment. It can request the medical record, including progress notes.",
        "question": "Can an insurance company ask for my psychotherapy notes?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Generally yes, under HIPAA's right of access, with limited exceptions.",
        "question": "Do clients have the right to see their progress notes?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "HHS says parents generally don't have a right to psychotherapy notes, though state law and other access rules may apply to the rest of the record.",
        "question": "Can a parent get a child's psychotherapy notes?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Only if they meet the definition and are kept separate from the medical record.",
        "question": "Are notes in my EHR's private section psychotherapy notes?"
      }
    ],
    "audience_tags": [
      "provider"
    ],
