    "slug": "telehealth-platform-work-pros-cons",
    "title": "Pros and Cons of Working for a Telehealth Therapy Platform",
    "primary_question": "What are the pros and cons of working for a telehealth therapy platform?",
    "summary": "What therapists gain and give up on telehealth therapy platforms: referral volume, matching, pay structure, clinical control, exclusivity, and support.",
    "body_markdown": "## What are the upsides of telehealth platforms?\n\n- **Referrals without marketing.** The platform brings clients to you.\n- **Remote work** with tools already set up.\n- **Fast start** compared with building a private practice.\n- **Flexibility,** at least on some platforms.\n\nFor new clinicians or therapists who hate marketing, that can be a real relief.\n\n## What are the common downsides?\n\nThey vary a lot by company, but therapists often mention:\n\n- **Pay structure.** Per-session or per-message pay, sometimes lower than other settings, sometimes with pay tiers tied to volume.\n- **Volume expectations.** Minimum hours or sessions, or slower referrals if you fall below them.\n- **Clinical constraints.** Rules on session length, messaging response times, or treatment approaches.\n- **Matching you don't control.** Clients assigned by algorithm, sometimes outside your specialty.\n- **Exclusivity or restrictions** on seeing clients elsewhere.\n- **Limited support** when something goes wrong, especially billing or technical problems.\n\n## What should you ask a platform?\n\n- How is pay calculated, and when is it paid?\n- Are there minimum hours or sessions?\n- Who decides session frequency and treatment approach?\n- Can I decline a match that isn't a fit?\n- Are messaging or async duties included, and are they paid?\n- What happens with no-shows?\n- Is the contract exclusive, and are there non-solicitation terms?\n- Who carries malpractice?\n\nSee [Questions to Ask Before Joining a Practice or Platform](/resources/for-therapists/questions-before-joining-therapy-platform).\n\n## How do platforms compare with other remote options?\n\n| | Platform | Supported network | Own telehealth practice |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Referrals | Platform matching | Network intake | You market |\n| Admin and billing | Platform | Network | You |\n| Clinical control | Varies | Usually yours | Yours |\n| Pay control | None | None | Full |\n| Exclusivity | Varies | Varies | None |\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nValorWell is a telehealth-first supported network for therapists serving Veterans and their families. You make the clinical decisions, set your availability and capacity, and can decline referrals that aren't a fit. There are no arbitrary productivity targets. ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims, and pays weekly for documented sessions. Denials don't come out of your pay, and when a client can be billed a no-show fee, the full amount goes to you. VA Community Care doesn't allow no-show charges, so ValorWell limits how many sessions those clients can miss. The arrangement is nonexclusive.\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **ValorWell serves a specific population:** Veterans, spouses, and children of Veterans, through CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- **No benefits and no malpractice coverage;** it's a 1099 role.\n- **Referrals depend on demand** in the states where you're licensed.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "They can be a fast way to build experience and income, especially if you'd rather not market. Read the contract carefully.",
        "question": "Are telehealth platforms good for new therapists?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Policies differ. Ask whether async messaging is paid and how response times are measured.",
        "question": "Do platforms pay for messaging with clients?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "If the contract is nonexclusive. Check for non-solicitation and conflict-of-interest terms.",
        "question": "Can I use a platform and keep a private practice?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Usually the platform. Confirm how clients and records are handled if you leave.",
        "question": "Who owns client records on a platform?"
      }
    ],
