    "slug": "client-self-scheduling-therapists",
    "title": "Should Therapists Let Clients Self-Schedule Appointments?",
    "primary_question": "Should therapists let clients self-schedule, and how much control should they keep over their calendar?",
    "summary": "Self-scheduling can save time and fill openings, but it gives up some control. How to decide, set boundaries, and keep control of your calendar.",
    "body_markdown": "## Should you let clients book their own sessions?\n\nFor many therapists, yes, with limits. Self-scheduling cuts down on messages, lets clients book when they think of it, and fills cancellations faster. The tradeoff is control: you may not love who lands in your last slot of the day, or a session booked an hour before.\n\n## What are the pros and cons?\n\n| Self-scheduling | Therapist-booked |\n| --- | --- |\n| Fewer emails and calls | More back-and-forth |\n| Openings fill faster | Openings may sit empty |\n| Clients feel more in control | You control the shape of your day |\n| Can lead to last-minute bookings | Lets you match sessions to your energy and acuity |\n| Easy rescheduling may reduce no-shows | Rescheduling takes your time |\n\n## How do you keep control while allowing it?\n\n- **Limit it to existing clients.** Keep intakes therapist-booked so you can screen for fit.\n- **Set a booking window,** such as no bookings less than 24 hours out.\n- **Show only the slots you want to fill.** Your calendar, your rules.\n- **Pair it with a cancellation policy** and automated reminders.\n- **Watch for patterns.** A client who books and cancels repeatedly is a clinical conversation.\n\n## Does self-scheduling raise privacy concerns?\n\nIt can. Use a scheduling tool that's part of a HIPAA-compliant system, with a business associate agreement where required. Avoid tools that send session details in unsecured emails or texts. See [HHS's HIPAA guidance](https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/laws-regulations/index.html).\n\n## When is therapist-booked better?\n\n- New clients, especially when screening matters.\n- Higher-risk clients who need consistent times.\n- Therapists with very limited availability.\n- Some couples or family sessions that need careful planning.\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nIn ValorWell's EHR, each therapist chooses whether clients can self-schedule or must contact them to book. You set your own availability, and you can switch between accepting and not accepting new clients at any time. The choice is yours, and you can change it.\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Self-scheduling doesn't replace clinical judgment** about frequency and timing.\n- **Authorizations still apply.** For VA Community Care clients, sessions must fall inside the approved dates and visit count.\n- **No tool is perfect.** Check bookings regularly, especially early on.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Many do, since it's convenient. Others prefer to talk with you. Offering both works well.",
        "question": "Do clients prefer self-scheduling?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "It can make rescheduling easier, which may help. Reminders and a clear cancellation policy matter more.",
        "question": "Does self-scheduling reduce no-shows?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Many therapists say no, so they can screen for fit first. It's your call.",
        "question": "Should new clients be able to self-schedule an intake?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "It can be, if the tool is set up for it, including a business associate agreement where required.",
        "question": "Is online scheduling HIPAA compliant?"
      }
    ],
