    "slug": "va-disability-letters-therapists",
    "title": "Should Therapists Write Letters for a Veteran's VA Disability Claim?",
    "primary_question": "Should therapists write letters for a veteran client's VA disability claim?",
    "summary": "Treating therapists can provide records and factual statements for a VA disability claim, but nexus opinions raise role conflicts. Here's how to decide.",
    "body_markdown": "## Can a treating therapist write a letter for a VA claim?\n\nYes. Nothing stops you, and VA accepts evidence from private providers. The real question is what kind of letter, and whether writing it fits your role.\n\nVA says a disability claim needs evidence of a current condition, an event or injury in service, and a link between them. That link usually comes from medical records or medical opinions. See [VA's evidence page](https://www.va.gov/disability/how-to-file-claim/evidence-needed/).\n\n## What's the difference between a treatment summary and a nexus opinion?\n\n| | Treatment summary | Nexus (medical) opinion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| What it says | Diagnosis, symptoms, functional effects, and treatment, from your records | Whether a condition is \"at least as likely as not\" caused by service |\n| What it requires | Your own clinical observations | A review of service and medical records, and a forensic judgment |\n| Role | Treating clinician | Evaluator |\n| Risk to therapy | Low | Can be significant |\n\nA treatment summary sticks to what you know. A nexus opinion asks you to judge causation, often about events you didn't witness, in a process where your client has a financial stake in the answer.\n\n## Why do many therapists avoid nexus opinions?\n\n- **Dual roles.** Professional ethics codes caution against mixing treatment and forensic roles. Your client needs you as their therapist, not their evaluator.\n- **The relationship.** If the claim is denied, or your opinion doesn't say what the client hoped, therapy can suffer.\n- **Scope.** A causation opinion may require records review and expertise outside your training.\n\nNone of that means a treating therapist can never write one. It means think it through, check your licensing board's and professional association's guidance, and decide deliberately.\n\n## What can you offer instead?\n\n- **Records.** Clients can request their records and submit them, or VA can request them with the client's authorization.\n- **A factual summary** of diagnosis, symptoms, treatment, and how the condition affects daily life and work.\n- **A referral** to an accredited Veterans Service Officer (VSO), who can help with the claim at no cost, or to an independent evaluator for a nexus opinion.\n- **The VA exam.** VA usually schedules its own compensation and pension (C&P) exam.\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nWhether to write a letter, and what kind, is your clinical and ethical decision. ValorWell doesn't require therapists to write disability opinions. Clients can request their records through the normal records process.\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **This isn't legal advice.** VA's evidence rules are detailed, and accredited VSOs, agents, and attorneys handle claims.\n- **Never overstate.** Write only what your records support.\n- **Get a signed release** before sending anything to VA or a representative.\n- **VA decides the claim.** Your letter is evidence, not a decision.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Yes. VA considers private medical evidence, including records and opinions from treating providers.",
        "question": "Does VA accept letters from private therapists?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "It's the standard VA uses for nexus opinions: a 50% or greater probability that the condition is related to service.",
        "question": "What does \"at least as likely as not\" mean?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Many therapists charge for reports outside sessions, since insurance doesn't cover them. Disclose any fee upfront and in writing.",
        "question": "Can I charge for writing a disability letter?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Accredited Veterans Service Officers from VA-recognized organizations help with claims at no cost.",
        "question": "Who can help a veteran with their claim for free?"
      }
    ],
