    "slug": "telehealth-client-location-emergency-plan",
    "title": "Telehealth Client Location and Emergency Planning for Therapists",
    "primary_question": "How should telehealth therapists confirm client location and plan for emergencies?",
    "summary": "How telehealth therapists confirm client location each session and plan for emergencies: addresses, emergency contacts, local resources, and 988.",
    "body_markdown": "## What should every telehealth emergency plan include?\n\nIf a client is in crisis now, connect them with the [Veterans Crisis Line](https://www.veteranscrisisline.net/) (dial 988, then press 1, or text 838255) or call 911 for immediate danger.\n\nFor planning ahead, gather these at intake and keep them current:\n\n- **The client's usual address** and the address of anywhere they often join from.\n- **An emergency contact** the client authorizes you to call.\n- **Local emergency resources:** the nearest emergency room and the local emergency number.\n- **A phone number** to reach the client if video drops.\n- **The client's consent** to the plan, documented.\n\n## Why confirm location every session?\n\nTwo reasons. First, licensure: you can only treat a client located in a state where you're licensed. Second, safety: if something goes wrong, you need to know where to send help. A client who usually logs on from home may be in a car, at work, or in another state.\n\nA simple script: \"Before we start, can you confirm where you are right now, and that you're somewhere private?\"\n\n## What if the connection drops?\n\nAgree in advance:\n\n1. You'll call the client's phone within a set number of minutes.\n2. If you can't reach them and you're worried about safety, you'll follow your emergency plan.\n3. If there's no safety concern, you'll reschedule.\n\n## What if a client is at risk during a session?\n\nStay connected, assess, and follow your protocol: involve the emergency contact, connect them to crisis services, or call emergency services to their confirmed location. Afterward, document what happened and consult if needed. VA's free [Suicide Risk Management Consultation Program](https://www.mirecc.va.gov/visn19/consult/how-it-works.asp) helps community providers think through risk with Veterans.\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nValorWell is a scheduled outpatient telehealth service, not a crisis service. ValorWell therapists follow standard telehealth safety practices, including confirming client location each session and having a plan for emergencies. Clinical decisions about risk stay with you, within your training and your licensing board's standards. ValorWell's admin team can help with coordination around a client's care.\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Emergency response depends on location.** Rural response times can be long; plan accordingly.\n- **State laws differ** on duty to warn and involuntary holds. Know the rules where your client is.\n- **Telehealth isn't right for every client.** Clients with high, ongoing risk may need a higher level of care.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Yes. Ask every session. People travel, and you need to know where to send help.",
        "question": "Do I need to verify location if a client always joins from home?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Local 911 routes by the caller's location. For a client elsewhere, use the local emergency number for their area.",
        "question": "Can I call 911 for a client in another state?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "No. 988 serves anyone. Veterans, service members, and families can press 1 to reach the Veterans Crisis Line.",
        "question": "Is 988 only for Veterans?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Risks and benefits, privacy, what happens if the connection drops, emergency procedures, and the client's location responsibilities.",
        "question": "What should a telehealth consent form cover?"
      }
    ],
