    "slug": "therapist-opportunities-california",
    "title": "Telehealth Therapist Jobs Serving Veterans in California",
    "primary_question": "What opportunities are there for licensed therapists to serve veterans and military families by telehealth in California?",
    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in California to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in California?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in California. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 1,381,390 Veterans live in California, the second-largest Veteran population of any state, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in California?\n\nThe independent counselor license in California is the **LPCC** (Licensed Professional Clinical Counselor), issued by the [California Board of Behavioral Sciences](https://www.bbs.ca.gov/). Associate Professional Clinical Counselors (APCC) practice under supervision, and ValorWell isn't working with supervised clinicians right now. Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in California also qualify, under the titles California uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in California, you need a California license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in California through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** California isn't a member as of October 2026, so counselors need a California license.\n- **PSYPACT:** California isn't participating as of 2026, so psychologists need a California license.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** California wasn't listed as a member on the compact's jurisdiction page as of 2026.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in California?\n\nVA medical centers serving California include facilities in Los Angeles, San Diego, San Francisco, Palo Alto, Long Beach, Loma Linda, Fresno, and Sacramento. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nCalifornia is in Community Care Network Region 4, run by TriWest. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. California isn't part of the Counseling Compact or PSYPACT, so therapists need a California license to see clients located there.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in California?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Yes, if you hold a California license or a valid compact privilege. What matters is where the client is located during the session.",
        "question": "Can I see ValorWell clients in California if I live in another state?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes. ValorWell accepts the independent LPCC license, along with LMFTs, independently licensed clinical social workers, and psychologists.",
        "question": "Does ValorWell accept LPCCs in California?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Community Care Network Region 4, run by TriWest, under VA's current contracts.",
        "question": "Which VA network region is California in?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans, covered through CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, or the ValorWell Foundation. You set the youngest age you'll see.",
        "question": "Who are the clients in California?"
      }
    ],
