    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in Massachusetts to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Massachusetts?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Massachusetts. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 255,345 Veterans live in Massachusetts, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Massachusetts?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Massachusetts is the **LMHC** (Licensed Mental Health Counselor), issued by the [Massachusetts Board of Registration of Allied Mental Health and Human Services Professions](https://www.mass.gov/how-to/apply-for-a-license-allied-mental-health-and-human-services-professions). Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Massachusetts also qualify, under the titles Massachusetts uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Massachusetts, you need a Massachusetts license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Massachusetts through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Massachusetts isn't a member as of October 2026, so counselors need a Massachusetts license.\n- **PSYPACT:** Sources differ on Massachusetts's status in 2026. Check the [PSYPACT site](https://psypact.org/) before relying on it.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Massachusetts wasn't listed as a member on the compact's jurisdiction page as of 2026.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Massachusetts?\n\nVA medical centers serving Massachusetts include facilities in Bedford, Boston (Jamaica Plain and West Roxbury), Brockton, and Leeds (Northampton). Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nMassachusetts is in Community Care Network Region 1, run by Optum. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Massachusetts?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
