    "primary_question": "What opportunities are there for licensed therapists to serve veterans and military families by telehealth in New Mexico?",
    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in New Mexico to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in New Mexico?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in New Mexico. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 148,473 Veterans live in New Mexico, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in New Mexico?\n\nThe independent counselor license in New Mexico is the **LPCC** (Licensed Professional Clinical Counselor), issued by the [New Mexico Counseling and Therapy Practice Board](https://www.rld.nm.gov/boards-and-commissions/individual-boards-and-commissions/counseling-and-therapy-practice/). In New Mexico, the LMHC is the earlier license and the LPCC is the independent clinical license. ValorWell works with the independent clinical license. Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in New Mexico also qualify, under the titles New Mexico uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in New Mexico, you need a New Mexico license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in New Mexico through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** New Mexico isn't a member as of October 2026, so counselors need a New Mexico license.\n- **PSYPACT:** New Mexico isn't participating as of 2026, so psychologists need a New Mexico license.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** New Mexico wasn't listed as a member on the compact's jurisdiction page as of 2026.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in New Mexico?\n\nVA medical centers serving New Mexico include facilities in Albuquerque. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nNew Mexico is in Community Care Network Region 4, run by TriWest. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. New Mexico is largely rural, and isn't part of the Counseling Compact or PSYPACT as of 2026.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in New Mexico?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
