    "slug": "therapist-opportunities-north-carolina",
    "title": "Telehealth Therapist Jobs Serving Veterans in North Carolina",
    "primary_question": "What opportunities are there for licensed therapists to serve veterans and military families by telehealth in North Carolina?",
    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in North Carolina to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in North Carolina?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in North Carolina. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 675,487 Veterans live in North Carolina, the fourth-largest Veteran population of any state, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in North Carolina?\n\nThe independent counselor license in North Carolina is the **LCMHC** (Licensed Clinical Mental Health Counselor), issued by the [North Carolina Board of Licensed Clinical Mental Health Counselors](https://sosnc.gov/divisions/licensing_boards/Board_of_Licensed_Clinical_Mental_Health_Counselors). Licensed Clinical Mental Health Counselor Associates (LCMHCA) practice under supervision, and ValorWell isn't working with supervised clinicians right now. Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in North Carolina also qualify, under the titles North Carolina uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in North Carolina, you need a North Carolina license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in North Carolina through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** North Carolina has enacted the compact but wasn't yet listed as issuing privileges as of October 2026. Until it does, counselors need a North Carolina license.\n- **PSYPACT:** North Carolina participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** North Carolina has enacted it, but the compact isn't issuing multistate licenses yet.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in North Carolina?\n\nVA medical centers serving North Carolina include facilities in Durham, Fayetteville, Asheville, and Salisbury. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nNorth Carolina is in Community Care Network Region 1, run by Optum. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. North Carolina also has a large active-duty military presence.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in North Carolina?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
