    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Pennsylvania?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Pennsylvania. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 646,936 Veterans live in Pennsylvania, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Pennsylvania?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Pennsylvania is the **LPC** (Licensed Professional Counselor), issued by the [Pennsylvania State Board of Social Workers, Marriage and Family Therapists and Professional Counselors](https://www.pa.gov/agencies/dos/department-and-offices/bpoa/boards-commissions/social-workers-marriage-family-therapists-professional-counselors). Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Pennsylvania also qualify, under the titles Pennsylvania uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Pennsylvania, you need a Pennsylvania license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Pennsylvania through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Pennsylvania has enacted the compact but wasn't yet listed as issuing privileges as of October 2026. Until it does, counselors need a Pennsylvania license.\n- **PSYPACT:** Pennsylvania participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Pennsylvania wasn't listed as a member on the compact's jurisdiction page as of 2026.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Pennsylvania?\n\nVA medical centers serving Pennsylvania include facilities in Philadelphia, Pittsburgh, Lebanon, Coatesville, Erie, Altoona, Butler, and Wilkes-Barre. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nPennsylvania is in Community Care Network Region 1, run by Optum. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. Pennsylvania joined the Counseling Compact in 2026.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Pennsylvania?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Yes, if you hold a Pennsylvania license or a valid compact privilege. What matters is where the client is located during the session.",
        "question": "Can I see ValorWell clients in Pennsylvania if I live in another state?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes. ValorWell accepts the independent LPC license, along with LMFTs, independently licensed clinical social workers, and psychologists.",
        "question": "Does ValorWell accept LPCs in Pennsylvania?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Community Care Network Region 1, run by Optum, under VA's current contracts.",
        "question": "Which VA network region is Pennsylvania in?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans, covered through CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, or the ValorWell Foundation. You set the youngest age you'll see.",
        "question": "Who are the clients in Pennsylvania?"
      }
    ],
