    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in Rhode Island to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Rhode Island?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Rhode Island. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 51,600 Veterans live in Rhode Island, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf). It's a smaller population, but providers who take VA programs are scarcer too.\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Rhode Island?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Rhode Island is the **LMHC** (Licensed Mental Health Counselor), issued by the [Rhode Island Board of Mental Health Counselors and Marriage and Family Therapists, part of RIDOH](https://health.ri.gov/). Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Rhode Island also qualify, under the titles Rhode Island uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Rhode Island, you need a Rhode Island license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Rhode Island through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Rhode Island has enacted the compact but wasn't yet listed as issuing privileges as of October 2026. Until it does, counselors need a Rhode Island license.\n- **PSYPACT:** Rhode Island participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Rhode Island has enacted it, but the compact isn't issuing multistate licenses yet.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Rhode Island?\n\nVA medical centers serving Rhode Island include facilities in Providence. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nRhode Island is in Community Care Network Region 1, run by Optum. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Rhode Island?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
