    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in South Dakota to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in South Dakota?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in South Dakota. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 62,615 Veterans live in South Dakota, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf). It's a smaller population, but providers who take VA programs are scarcer too.\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in South Dakota?\n\nThe independent counselor license in South Dakota is the **LPC-MH** (Licensed Professional Counselor-Mental Health), issued by the [South Dakota Board of Examiners for Counselors and Marriage and Family Therapists](https://dss.sd.gov/licensingboards/counselors/counselors.aspx). South Dakota also licenses LPCs; the LPC-MH designation is the clinical mental health license. ValorWell works with the independent clinical license. Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in South Dakota also qualify, under the titles South Dakota uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in South Dakota, you need a South Dakota license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in South Dakota through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** South Dakota has enacted the compact but wasn't yet listed as issuing privileges as of October 2026. Until it does, counselors need a South Dakota license.\n- **PSYPACT:** South Dakota participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** South Dakota has enacted it, but the compact isn't issuing multistate licenses yet.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in South Dakota?\n\nVA medical centers serving South Dakota include facilities in Sioux Falls, Fort Meade, and Hot Springs. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nSouth Dakota is in Community Care Network Region 2, run by Optum. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. Much of South Dakota is rural and frontier, including tribal lands far from VA facilities.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in South Dakota?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
