    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Washington?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Washington. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 494,383 Veterans live in Washington, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Washington?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Washington is the **LMHC** (Licensed Mental Health Counselor), issued by the [Washington State Department of Health](https://doh.wa.gov/licenses-permits-and-certificates/professions-new-renew-or-update/mental-health-counselor). Licensed Mental Health Counselor Associates practice under supervision, and ValorWell isn't working with supervised clinicians right now. Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Washington also qualify, under the titles Washington uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Washington, you need a Washington license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Washington through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Washington has enacted the compact but wasn't yet listed as issuing privileges as of October 2026. Until it does, counselors need a Washington license.\n- **PSYPACT:** Washington participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Washington has enacted it, but the compact isn't issuing multistate licenses yet.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Washington?\n\nVA medical centers serving Washington include facilities in Seattle, Tacoma (American Lake), Spokane, Walla Walla, and Vancouver. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nWashington is in Community Care Network Region 4, run by TriWest. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. Eastern Washington is largely rural, far from the Puget Sound medical centers.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Washington?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Yes, if you hold a Washington license or a valid compact privilege. What matters is where the client is located during the session.",
        "question": "Can I see ValorWell clients in Washington if I live in another state?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes. ValorWell accepts the independent LMHC license, along with LMFTs, independently licensed clinical social workers, and psychologists.",
        "question": "Does ValorWell accept LMHCs in Washington?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Community Care Network Region 4, run by TriWest, under VA's current contracts.",
        "question": "Which VA network region is Washington in?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans, covered through CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, or the ValorWell Foundation. You set the youngest age you'll see.",
        "question": "Who are the clients in Washington?"
      }
    ],
