    "primary_question": "What opportunities are there for licensed therapists to serve veterans and military families by telehealth in Alaska?",
    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in Alaska to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Alaska?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Alaska. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 69,063 Veterans live in Alaska, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Alaska?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Alaska is the **LPC** (Licensed Professional Counselor), issued by the [Alaska Board of Professional Counselors](https://commerce.alaska.gov/web/CBPL/ProfessionalLicensing/ProfessionalCounselors.aspx). Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Alaska also qualify, under the titles Alaska uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Alaska, you need an Alaska license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Alaska through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Alaska isn't a member as of October 2026, so counselors need an Alaska license.\n- **PSYPACT:** Alaska isn't participating as of 2026, so psychologists need an Alaska license.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Alaska has enacted it, but the compact isn't issuing multistate licenses yet.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Alaska?\n\nVA medical centers serving Alaska include facilities in Anchorage, through the Alaska VA Healthcare System. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nAlaska is in Community Care Network Region 5, run by TriWest. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. Alaska has no full-service VA hospital. The Alaska VA Healthcare System partners with the military hospital at Joint Base Elmendorf-Richardson for inpatient care, and distances across the state make community care and telehealth especially important.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Alaska?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Yes, if you hold an Alaska license or a valid compact privilege. What matters is where the client is located during the session.",
        "question": "Can I see ValorWell clients in Alaska if I live in another state?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes. ValorWell accepts the independent LPC license, along with LMFTs, independently licensed clinical social workers, and psychologists.",
        "question": "Does ValorWell accept LPCs in Alaska?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Community Care Network Region 5, run by TriWest, under VA's current contracts.",
        "question": "Which VA network region is Alaska in?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans, covered through CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, or the ValorWell Foundation. You set the youngest age you'll see.",
        "question": "Who are the clients in Alaska?"
      }
    ],
