    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Arizona?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Arizona. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 467,010 Veterans live in Arizona, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Arizona?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Arizona is the **LPC** (Licensed Professional Counselor), issued by the Arizona Board of Behavioral Health Examiners. Licensed Associate Counselors (LAC) practice under supervision, and ValorWell isn't working with supervised clinicians right now. Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Arizona also qualify, under the titles Arizona uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Arizona, you need an Arizona license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Arizona through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Arizona is a member state and is issuing privileges to practice, so counselors whose home state is also an active member can apply for a privilege to practice in Arizona.\n- **PSYPACT:** Arizona participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Arizona has enacted it, but the compact isn't issuing multistate licenses yet.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Arizona?\n\nVA medical centers serving Arizona include facilities in Phoenix, Tucson, and Prescott. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nArizona is in Community Care Network Region 4, run by TriWest. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Arizona?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
