    "primary_question": "What opportunities are there for licensed therapists to serve veterans and military families by telehealth in the District of Columbia?",
    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in D.C. to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in D.C.?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in the District of Columbia. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 26,567 Veterans live in the District of Columbia, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in D.C.?\n\nThe independent counselor license in D.C. is the **LPC** (Licensed Professional Counselor), issued by the [DC Board of Professional Counseling, part of DC Health](https://dchealth.dc.gov/service/licensing-boards). Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in D.C. also qualify, under the titles D.C. uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in D.C., you need a D.C. license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in D.C. through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** The District of Columbia has enacted the compact but wasn't yet listed as issuing privileges as of October 2026. Until it does, counselors need a D.C. license.\n- **PSYPACT:** The District of Columbia participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** The District of Columbia wasn't listed as a member on the compact's jurisdiction page as of 2026.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in D.C.?\n\nVA medical centers serving the District of Columbia include facilities in Washington. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nD.C. is in Community Care Network Region 1, run by Optum. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. D.C. is small, and many clients' daily lives cross into Maryland and Virginia. Check where the client is located for each session.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in D.C.?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Yes, if you hold a D.C. license or a valid compact privilege. What matters is where the client is located during the session.",
        "question": "Can I see ValorWell clients in D.C. if I live in another state?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes. ValorWell accepts the independent LPC license, along with LMFTs, independently licensed clinical social workers, and psychologists.",
        "question": "Does ValorWell accept LPCs in D.C.?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Community Care Network Region 1, run by Optum, under VA's current contracts.",
        "question": "Which VA network region is D.C. in?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans, covered through CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, or the ValorWell Foundation. You set the youngest age you'll see.",
        "question": "Who are the clients in D.C.?"
      }
    ],
