    "primary_question": "What opportunities are there for licensed therapists to serve veterans and military families by telehealth in Delaware?",
    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in Delaware to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Delaware?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Delaware. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 61,773 Veterans live in Delaware, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf). It's a smaller population, but providers who take VA programs are scarcer too.\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Delaware?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Delaware is the **LPCMH** (Licensed Professional Counselor of Mental Health), issued by the [Delaware Board of Mental Health and Chemical Dependency Professionals](https://dpr.delaware.gov/boards/profcounselors). Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Delaware also qualify, under the titles Delaware uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Delaware, you need a Delaware license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Delaware through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Delaware has enacted the compact but wasn't yet listed as issuing privileges as of October 2026. Until it does, counselors need a Delaware license.\n- **PSYPACT:** Delaware participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Delaware has enacted it, but the compact isn't issuing multistate licenses yet.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Delaware?\n\nVA medical centers serving Delaware include facilities in Wilmington. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nDelaware is in Community Care Network Region 1, run by Optum. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Delaware?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
