    "primary_question": "What opportunities are there for licensed therapists to serve veterans and military families by telehealth in Hawaii?",
    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in Hawaii to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Hawaii?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Hawaii. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 105,848 Veterans live in Hawaii, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Hawaii?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Hawaii is the **LMHC** (Licensed Mental Health Counselor), issued by the [Hawaii Mental Health Counselor Program, Professional and Vocational Licensing](https://cca.hawaii.gov/pvl/). Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Hawaii also qualify, under the titles Hawaii uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Hawaii, you need a Hawaii license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Hawaii through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Hawaii isn't a member as of October 2026, so counselors need a Hawaii license.\n- **PSYPACT:** Hawaii isn't participating as of 2026, so psychologists need a Hawaii license.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Hawaii wasn't listed as a member on the compact's jurisdiction page as of 2026.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Hawaii?\n\nVA medical centers serving Hawaii include facilities in Honolulu (Spark M. Matsunaga VA Medical Center). Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nHawaii is in Community Care Network Region 4, run by TriWest. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. Hawaii has no full-service VA hospital. VA works with Tripler Army Medical Center for inpatient care, and Veterans on neighbor islands depend heavily on community care and telehealth.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Hawaii?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Yes, if you hold a Hawaii license or a valid compact privilege. What matters is where the client is located during the session.",
        "question": "Can I see ValorWell clients in Hawaii if I live in another state?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes. ValorWell accepts the independent LMHC license, along with LMFTs, independently licensed clinical social workers, and psychologists.",
        "question": "Does ValorWell accept LMHCs in Hawaii?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Community Care Network Region 4, run by TriWest, under VA's current contracts.",
        "question": "Which VA network region is Hawaii in?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans, covered through CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, or the ValorWell Foundation. You set the youngest age you'll see.",
        "question": "Who are the clients in Hawaii?"
      }
    ],
