    "slug": "therapist-opportunities-idaho",
    "title": "Therapist Jobs Serving Veterans in Idaho: Telehealth Opportunities",
    "primary_question": "What opportunities are there for licensed therapists to serve veterans and military families by telehealth in Idaho?",
    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in Idaho to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Idaho?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Idaho. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 121,295 Veterans live in Idaho, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Idaho?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Idaho is the **LCPC** (Licensed Clinical Professional Counselor), issued by the [Idaho Board of Professional Counselors and Marriage and Family Therapists](https://dopl.idaho.gov/cou/). In Idaho, the LPC is the earlier license and the LCPC is the independent clinical license. ValorWell works with the independent clinical license. Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Idaho also qualify, under the titles Idaho uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Idaho, you need an Idaho license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Idaho through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Idaho isn't a member as of October 2026, so counselors need an Idaho license.\n- **PSYPACT:** Idaho participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Idaho wasn't listed as a member on the compact's jurisdiction page as of 2026.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Idaho?\n\nVA medical centers serving Idaho include facilities in Boise. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nIdaho is in Community Care Network Region 4, run by TriWest. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. Much of Idaho is rural and far from Boise, so telehealth fills real gaps.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Idaho?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
