    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in Indiana to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Indiana?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Indiana. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 356,627 Veterans live in Indiana, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Indiana?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Indiana is the **LMHC** (Licensed Mental Health Counselor), issued by the [Indiana Behavioral Health and Human Services Licensing Board](https://www.in.gov/pla/professions/behavioral-health-and-human-services/behavioral-health-and-human-services-licensing-board-board). LMHC Associates practice under supervision, and ValorWell isn't working with supervised clinicians right now. Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Indiana also qualify, under the titles Indiana uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Indiana, you need an Indiana license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Indiana through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Indiana is a member state and is issuing privileges to practice, so counselors whose home state is also an active member can apply for a privilege to practice in Indiana.\n- **PSYPACT:** Indiana participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Indiana wasn't listed as a member on the compact's jurisdiction page as of 2026.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Indiana?\n\nVA medical centers serving Indiana include facilities in Indianapolis, Marion, and Fort Wayne. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nIndiana is in Community Care Network Region 2, run by Optum. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Indiana?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
