    "primary_question": "What opportunities are there for licensed therapists to serve veterans and military families by telehealth in Iowa?",
    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in Iowa to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Iowa?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Iowa. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 172,693 Veterans live in Iowa, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Iowa?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Iowa is the **LMHC** (Licensed Mental Health Counselor), issued by the [Iowa Board of Behavioral Health Professionals](https://dial.iowa.gov/about-dial/boards-and-commissions/about-bhp-board). Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Iowa also qualify, under the titles Iowa uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Iowa, you need an Iowa license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Iowa through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Iowa has enacted the compact but wasn't yet listed as issuing privileges as of October 2026. Until it does, counselors need an Iowa license.\n- **PSYPACT:** Iowa isn't participating as of 2026, so psychologists need an Iowa license.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Iowa has enacted it, but the compact isn't issuing multistate licenses yet.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Iowa?\n\nVA medical centers serving Iowa include facilities in Des Moines and Iowa City. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nIowa is in Community Care Network Region 2, run by Optum. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. Iowa isn't a PSYPACT state as of 2026, so psychologists need an Iowa license to see clients there.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Iowa?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Yes, if you hold an Iowa license or a valid compact privilege. What matters is where the client is located during the session.",
        "question": "Can I see ValorWell clients in Iowa if I live in another state?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes. ValorWell accepts the independent LMHC license, along with LMFTs, independently licensed clinical social workers, and psychologists.",
        "question": "Does ValorWell accept LMHCs in Iowa?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Community Care Network Region 2, run by Optum, under VA's current contracts.",
        "question": "Which VA network region is Iowa in?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans, covered through CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, or the ValorWell Foundation. You set the youngest age you'll see.",
        "question": "Who are the clients in Iowa?"
      }
    ],
