    "slug": "therapist-opportunities-kansas",
    "title": "Therapist Jobs Serving Veterans in Kansas: Telehealth Opportunities",
    "primary_question": "What opportunities are there for licensed therapists to serve veterans and military families by telehealth in Kansas?",
    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in Kansas to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Kansas?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Kansas. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 174,200 Veterans live in Kansas, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Kansas?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Kansas is the **LCPC** (Licensed Clinical Professional Counselor), issued by the [Kansas Behavioral Sciences Regulatory Board](https://www.ksbsrb.ks.gov/professions/professional-counselors). Kansas also licenses LPCs; the LCPC is the clinical license that allows independent diagnosis and treatment. ValorWell works with the independent clinical license. Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Kansas also qualify, under the titles Kansas uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Kansas, you need a Kansas license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Kansas through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Kansas has enacted the compact but wasn't yet listed as issuing privileges as of October 2026. Until it does, counselors need a Kansas license.\n- **PSYPACT:** Kansas participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Kansas has enacted it, but the compact isn't issuing multistate licenses yet.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Kansas?\n\nVA medical centers serving Kansas include facilities in Topeka, Leavenworth, and Wichita. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nKansas is in Community Care Network Region 2, run by Optum. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. Western Kansas is largely rural, with long drives to any VA medical center.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Kansas?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
