    "slug": "therapist-opportunities-michigan",
    "title": "Therapist Jobs Serving Veterans in Michigan: Telehealth Opportunities",
    "primary_question": "What opportunities are there for licensed therapists to serve veterans and military families by telehealth in Michigan?",
    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in Michigan to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Michigan?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Michigan. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 473,290 Veterans live in Michigan, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Michigan?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Michigan is the **LPC** (Licensed Professional Counselor), issued by the [Michigan Board of Counseling, part of LARA](https://www.michigan.gov/lara). Limited Licensed Professional Counselors (LLPC) practice under supervision, and ValorWell isn't working with supervised clinicians right now. Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Michigan also qualify, under the titles Michigan uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Michigan, you need a Michigan license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Michigan through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Michigan isn't a member as of October 2026, so counselors need a Michigan license.\n- **PSYPACT:** Michigan participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Michigan wasn't listed as a member on the compact's jurisdiction page as of 2026.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Michigan?\n\nVA medical centers serving Michigan include facilities in Ann Arbor, Battle Creek, Detroit, Saginaw, and Iron Mountain. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nMichigan is in Community Care Network Region 2, run by Optum. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. The Upper Peninsula and northern Lower Peninsula are rural, with long distances to care.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Michigan?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
