    "primary_question": "What opportunities are there for licensed therapists to serve veterans and military families by telehealth in Minnesota?",
    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in Minnesota to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Minnesota?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Minnesota. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 277,912 Veterans live in Minnesota, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Minnesota?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Minnesota is the **LPCC** (Licensed Professional Clinical Counselor), issued by the [Minnesota Board of Behavioral Health and Therapy](https://mn.gov/boards/behavioral-health/). Minnesota also licenses LPCs; the LPCC is the clinical license. ValorWell works with the independent clinical license. Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Minnesota also qualify, under the titles Minnesota uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Minnesota, you need a Minnesota license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Minnesota through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Minnesota is a member state and is issuing privileges to practice, so counselors whose home state is also an active member can apply for a privilege to practice in Minnesota.\n- **PSYPACT:** Minnesota participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Minnesota has enacted it, but the compact isn't issuing multistate licenses yet.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Minnesota?\n\nVA medical centers serving Minnesota include facilities in Minneapolis and St. Cloud. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nMinnesota is in Community Care Network Region 2, run by Optum. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. Minnesota was one of the first two states to issue Counseling Compact privileges, in September 2025.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Minnesota?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
