    "primary_question": "What opportunities are there for licensed therapists to serve veterans and military families by telehealth in Missouri?",
    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in Missouri to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Missouri?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Missouri. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 377,618 Veterans live in Missouri, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Missouri?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Missouri is the **LPC** (Licensed Professional Counselor), issued by the [Missouri Committee for Professional Counselors](https://pr.mo.gov/). Provisional Licensed Professional Counselors (PLPC) practice under supervision, and ValorWell isn't working with supervised clinicians right now. Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Missouri also qualify, under the titles Missouri uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Missouri, you need a Missouri license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Missouri through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Missouri has enacted the compact but wasn't yet listed as issuing privileges as of October 2026. Until it does, counselors need a Missouri license.\n- **PSYPACT:** Missouri participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Missouri has enacted it, but the compact isn't issuing multistate licenses yet.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Missouri?\n\nVA medical centers serving Missouri include facilities in Kansas City, Columbia, St. Louis, and Poplar Bluff. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nMissouri is in Community Care Network Region 2, run by Optum. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Missouri?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Yes, if you hold a Missouri license or a valid compact privilege. What matters is where the client is located during the session.",
        "question": "Can I see ValorWell clients in Missouri if I live in another state?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes. ValorWell accepts the independent LPC license, along with LMFTs, independently licensed clinical social workers, and psychologists.",
        "question": "Does ValorWell accept LPCs in Missouri?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Community Care Network Region 2, run by Optum, under VA's current contracts.",
        "question": "Which VA network region is Missouri in?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans, covered through CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, or the ValorWell Foundation. You set the youngest age you'll see.",
        "question": "Who are the clients in Missouri?"
      }
    ],
