    "primary_question": "What opportunities are there for licensed therapists to serve veterans and military families by telehealth in Montana?",
    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in Montana to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Montana?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Montana. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 85,871 Veterans live in Montana, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Montana?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Montana is the **LCPC** (Licensed Clinical Professional Counselor), issued by the [Montana Board of Behavioral Health](https://boards.bsd.dli.mt.gov/behavioral-health/). Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Montana also qualify, under the titles Montana uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Montana, you need a Montana license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Montana through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Montana has enacted the compact but wasn't yet listed as issuing privileges as of October 2026. Until it does, counselors need a Montana license.\n- **PSYPACT:** Sources differ on Montana's status in 2026. Check the [PSYPACT site](https://psypact.org/) before relying on it.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Montana wasn't listed as a member on the compact's jurisdiction page as of 2026.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Montana?\n\nVA medical centers serving Montana include facilities in Fort Harrison, near Helena. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nMontana is in Community Care Network Region 4, run by TriWest. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. Montana is vast and largely rural, and many Veterans live hours from Fort Harrison.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Montana?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
