    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in Nebraska to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Nebraska?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Nebraska. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 114,944 Veterans live in Nebraska, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Nebraska?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Nebraska is the **LIMHP** (Licensed Independent Mental Health Practitioner), issued by the [Nebraska Department of Health and Human Services, Licensure Unit](https://dhhs.ne.gov/). Nebraska also licenses Mental Health Practitioners (LMHP); the LIMHP is the independent license. ValorWell works with the independent clinical license. Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Nebraska also qualify, under the titles Nebraska uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Nebraska, you need a Nebraska license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Nebraska through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Nebraska has enacted the compact but wasn't yet listed as issuing privileges as of October 2026. Until it does, counselors need a Nebraska license.\n- **PSYPACT:** Nebraska participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Nebraska has enacted it, but the compact isn't issuing multistate licenses yet.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Nebraska?\n\nVA medical centers serving Nebraska include facilities in Omaha and Grand Island. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nNebraska is in Community Care Network Region 2, run by Optum. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. Western Nebraska is rural, with long distances to VA care.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Nebraska?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
