    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in New York?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in New York. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 613,931 Veterans live in New York, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in New York?\n\nThe independent counselor license in New York is the **LMHC** (Licensed Mental Health Counselor), issued by the [New York State Education Department, Office of the Professions](https://www.op.nysed.gov/professions/mental-health-counselors). Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in New York also qualify, under the titles New York uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in New York, you need a New York license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in New York through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** New York isn't a member as of October 2026, so counselors need a New York license.\n- **PSYPACT:** New York isn't participating as of 2026, so psychologists need a New York license.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** New York wasn't listed as a member on the compact's jurisdiction page as of 2026.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in New York?\n\nVA medical centers serving New York include facilities in the Bronx, Manhattan, Brooklyn, Northport, Buffalo, Syracuse, Albany, and Canandaigua. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nNew York is in Community Care Network Region 1, run by Optum. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. New York isn't part of the Counseling Compact or PSYPACT, so therapists need a New York license to see clients there.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in New York?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Yes, if you hold a New York license or a valid compact privilege. What matters is where the client is located during the session.",
        "question": "Can I see ValorWell clients in New York if I live in another state?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes. ValorWell accepts the independent LMHC license, along with LMFTs, independently licensed clinical social workers, and psychologists.",
        "question": "Does ValorWell accept LMHCs in New York?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Community Care Network Region 1, run by Optum, under VA's current contracts.",
        "question": "Which VA network region is New York in?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans, covered through CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, or the ValorWell Foundation. You set the youngest age you'll see.",
        "question": "Who are the clients in New York?"
      }
    ],
