    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Texas?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Texas. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nVA estimates about 1,518,967 Veterans live in Texas, the largest Veteran population of any state, according to VA's [fiscal year 2025 Annual Benefits Report](https://benefits.va.gov/REPORTS/abr/docs/2025-abr.pdf).\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Texas?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Texas is the **LPC** (Licensed Professional Counselor), issued by the [Texas State Board of Examiners of Professional Counselors, part of the Behavioral Health Executive Council](https://bhec.texas.gov/texas-state-board-of-examiners-of-professional-counselors/applying-for-an-lpc-license/). LPC Associates practice under supervision, and ValorWell isn't working with supervised clinicians right now. Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Texas also qualify, under the titles Texas uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Texas, you need a Texas license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Texas through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Texas isn't a member as of October 2026, so counselors need a Texas license.\n- **PSYPACT:** Texas participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Texas wasn't listed as a member on the compact's jurisdiction page as of 2026.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Texas?\n\nVA medical centers serving Texas include facilities in Houston, Dallas, San Antonio, Temple, Waco, Amarillo, Big Spring, Bonham, and Kerrville. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nTexas is in Community Care Network Region 4, run by TriWest. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. Texas is large enough that many Veterans live hours from the nearest VA medical center.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Texas?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
