    "summary": "ValorWell needs licensed therapists in Wyoming to treat Veterans and their families by telehealth. Licenses, compacts, VA care, and how it works.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is ValorWell hiring therapists in Wyoming?\n\nYes. ValorWell has a great need for more licensed therapists in Wyoming. Veterans, Veteran spouses, and children of Veterans there are looking for therapists who accept CHAMPVA and VA Community Care, and there aren't enough.\n\nWyoming has one of the smallest Veteran populations in the country, but providers who take VA programs are scarce there too.\n\n## Which licenses can treat Veterans and families in Wyoming?\n\nThe independent counselor license in Wyoming is the **LPC** (Licensed Professional Counselor), issued by the [Wyoming Mental Health Professions Licensing Board](https://mentalhealth.wyo.gov/board-info). Provisional Professional Counselors practice under supervision, and ValorWell isn't working with supervised clinicians right now. Licensed marriage and family therapists, independently licensed clinical social workers, and licensed psychologists in Wyoming also qualify, under the titles Wyoming uses.\n\nFor telehealth, the license has to match where the client is sitting during the session. To see a client located in Wyoming, you need a Wyoming license or a valid compact privilege. See [Can Therapists See Telehealth Clients in Another State?](/resources/for-therapists/telehealth-licensure-client-location)\n\n## Can you practice in Wyoming through a compact?\n\n- **Counseling Compact:** Wyoming is a member state and is issuing privileges to practice, so counselors whose home state is also an active member can apply for a privilege to practice in Wyoming.\n- **PSYPACT:** Wyoming participates, so psychologists with PSYPACT authority can practice telepsychology with clients located there.\n- **Social Work Licensure Compact:** Wyoming wasn't listed as a member on the compact's jurisdiction page as of 2026.\n\nCompact status changes often. See [Counseling Compact, PSYPACT, and the Social Work Compact](/resources/for-therapists/counseling-compact-psypact) and the [Counseling Compact map](https://counselingcompact.org/map/compact-states/).\n\n## What VA care is available in Wyoming?\n\nVA medical centers serving Wyoming include facilities in Cheyenne and Sheridan. Use [VA's facility locator](https://www.va.gov/find-locations/) for clinics and Vet Centers near a client.\n\nWyoming is in Community Care Network Region 4, run by TriWest. VA has started moving to new network contracts, so regions and administrators may change. Wyoming is largely rural, with long distances between Veterans and VA care. It began issuing Counseling Compact privileges in 2026.\n\n## How does ValorWell work for therapists in Wyoming?\n\nValorWell is telehealth-first. You work from wherever you are, as a 1099 contractor:\n\n- You make the clinical decisions and set your availability and capacity.\n- ValorWell handles credentialing, authorizations, billing, and claims for CHAMPVA, VA Community Care, and the ValorWell Foundation.\n- You're paid weekly for sessions you documented, and denials don't come out of your pay.\n- The arrangement is nonexclusive, so you can keep a private practice too.\n\nFor the full picture, see [How ValorWell Works for Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/valorwell-model-for-therapists) and [What a Therapist Needs to Join ValorWell](/resources/for-therapists/therapist-requirements-valorwell).\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Need isn't a referral guarantee.** Volume depends on client eligibility, authorizations, and fit.\n- **Fully licensed only.** Associates and prescribers aren't a fit right now.\n- **Check the board.** License titles and rules can change; the state board is the authority.",
