    "slug": "unpaid-admin-work-after-hours",
    "title": "Unpaid Admin Work After Hours: What Therapists Can Do",
    "primary_question": "What can therapists do when documentation, calls, and admin spill into unpaid personal time?",
    "summary": "When notes, calls, and admin spill into unpaid time: what wage law says for W-2 therapists, what contractors control, and how to cut the admin load.",
    "body_markdown": "## Is after-hours admin work paid?\n\nIt depends on how you're classified:\n\n| Status | Is after-hours admin paid? |\n| --- | --- |\n| Non-exempt W-2 (hourly) | Yes. Hours worked include documentation and calls, and overtime rules apply. |\n| Exempt W-2 (salaried professional) | Not separately; the salary covers the work. |\n| Paid per session (W-2 or 1099) | Usually not; pay is tied to sessions. |\n| Independent contractor | You control your time; admin is part of your business. |\n\nUnder the Fair Labor Standards Act, whether a salaried employee is exempt from overtime depends on their duties and how and how much they're paid. Job titles alone don't decide it. See [DOL Fact Sheet 17D](https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/fact-sheets/17d-overtime-professional). If you think you're owed pay for hours worked, the [Wage and Hour Division](https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd) can help.\n\n## Where does the after-hours work come from?\n\n- Progress notes and treatment plans.\n- Calls with clients, families, and other providers.\n- Insurance work: eligibility checks, authorizations, claims, and denials.\n- Scheduling and rescheduling.\n- Email and messages.\n\nFor therapists in private practice, insurance work is often the biggest piece.\n\n## How can you shrink it?\n\n- **Block documentation time** into your schedule, and treat it as real work.\n- **Use better tools.** Templates and well-designed EHRs reduce note time.\n- **Hand off billing** to a billing service, a group practice, or a network.\n- **Set communication limits.** Tell clients when you respond to messages.\n- **Reduce sessions** if the admin can't fit into the hours you have.\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nValorWell takes most of the business admin off its therapists: credentialing, authorizations, billing, claims, payer follow-up, and denials. Scheduling runs through the EHR, and you can let clients book themselves. ValorWell's admin team handles coordination and generally responds within a few hours.\n\nDocumentation is still yours, but the EHR was built with clinician input and includes AI-assisted documentation. There's no official note deadline; you're paid for sessions whose notes are completed in the pay period, so you decide when to write.\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **Contractors aren't paid for admin time separately.** At ValorWell, pay is per documented session.\n- **Notes remain your responsibility.** AI assistance supports documentation; it doesn't replace clinical review.\n- **This isn't legal advice.** Wage and hour rules depend on facts and state law; talk to the DOL or an employment attorney for your situation.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Some salaried therapists qualify for the professional exemption, but it depends on duties and pay. Hourly therapists are generally entitled to overtime.",
        "question": "Are therapists exempt from overtime?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "For non-exempt employees, yes. Documentation is part of the job.",
        "question": "Should documentation time count as hours worked?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "For non-exempt employees, hours worked must be paid. For exempt employees and per-session pay, it depends on your contract and state law.",
        "question": "Can my employer require notes after hours without pay?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Contact the U.S. Department of Labor's Wage and Hour Division or your state labor department.",
        "question": "How do I report unpaid wages?"
      }
    ],
