    "status": "published",
    "published_at": "2026-10-14T18:43:34.260159+00:00",
    "public_updated_at": "2026-10-14T18:43:34.260159+00:00",
    "resource_kind": "article",
    "category_slug": "va-community-care",
    "sort_order": 1,
    "quick_answer": "If VA can't schedule primary care, mental health care, or non-institutional extended care within 20 days of your request and within a 30-minute average drive of your home, you qualify for Community Care. For specialty care, it's 28 days and 60 minutes. The clock starts when you ask, unless you agree to a later date.",
    "in_practice_markdown": "The 20-day standard is the one we see questioned most. People are told it doesn't exist, that it doesn't apply to their kind of care, or that it only counts for new patients. The regulation doesn't carve out existing patients; it counts from the date you request the care. The approach that works best for us is a polite Secure Message that points to the exact regulation and asks VA to confirm its position in writing. Full steps and a copy-and-paste message: [What to do if your Community Care referral is delayed or stuck](/resources/va-community-care/referral-delays-patient-advocate).",
    "reviewed_by": "ValorWell",
    "last_reviewed_on": null,
    "cta": "find_care",
