    "slug": "w2-vs-1099-therapist",
    "title": "W-2 vs. 1099 Therapist: What's the Real Difference?",
    "primary_question": "W-2 or 1099: what's the real difference for therapists?",
    "summary": "W-2 therapists are employees with benefits and less control; 1099 therapists are contractors with more control and their own taxes. How to compare.",
    "body_markdown": "## What's the difference between W-2 and 1099 for therapists?\n\n| | W-2 employee | 1099 independent contractor |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Taxes | Withheld by employer; employer pays half of Social Security and Medicare | You pay all of it, usually through quarterly estimates |\n| Benefits | Often health insurance, retirement, paid time off | None from the business; you buy your own |\n| Control over hours and methods | Employer can direct them | You largely control them |\n| Wage and hour protections | Minimum wage and overtime rules apply to non-exempt employees | Don't apply |\n| Malpractice | Often covered by employer | Usually your own |\n| Unemployment and workers' comp | Usually covered | Usually not |\n| Other work | May be restricted | Often allowed |\n\n## What decides whether you're really a contractor?\n\nHow the work actually happens, not what the contract calls it. The [IRS](https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/independent-contractor-self-employed-or-employee) looks at three kinds of evidence:\n\n- **Behavioral control:** does the business control what you do and how?\n- **Financial control:** who controls the business side, like pay, expenses, and tools?\n- **Relationship:** is there a written contract, are there benefits, and is the work ongoing and central to the business?\n\nThe Department of Labor uses an \"economic reality\" test under the Fair Labor Standards Act. In 2025, DOL said it would stop applying its 2024 rule in investigations, and in February 2026 it proposed a new rule that weighs control over the work and opportunity for profit or loss most heavily. See [DOL's rulemaking page](https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/flsa/misclassification/2026rulemaking). States can apply their own, stricter tests.\n\n## Which is better for autonomy?\n\nUsually 1099, because a business that tightly controls a contractor's hours and methods is acting like an employer. A real contractor role should leave you in charge of your schedule and clinical approach.\n\nBut W-2 roles can be excellent too: benefits, paid leave, and steady pay matter. The best choice depends on your stage of life and what you value.\n\n## How do you compare offers?\n\nDon't compare a W-2 salary to 1099 per-session pay directly. On the 1099 side, account for self-employment tax, health insurance, retirement savings, malpractice, unpaid time off, and business expenses. See [How Taxes Work for 1099 Therapists](/resources/for-therapists/1099-therapist-taxes).\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nValorWell therapists are 1099 independent contractors. The arrangement is nonexclusive, and you control your availability, capacity, and clinical decisions. ValorWell handles the business infrastructure for its pathways, such as credentialing and billing, but doesn't direct how you do clinical work.\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **No W-2 benefits.** Health insurance, retirement, and paid time off are on you.\n- **You handle your own taxes.** Nothing is withheld.\n- **You carry your own malpractice coverage.**\n- **This isn't legal or tax advice.** Classification depends on facts and state law.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Yes, with different organizations. Many therapists hold a W-2 job and do contract work on the side.",
        "question": "Can a therapist be both W-2 and 1099?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Financially, it shifts more responsibility to you: taxes, benefits, and income ups and downs. Many find the control worth it.",
        "question": "Is being 1099 riskier?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "That may be misclassification. You can ask the IRS for a status determination with Form SS-8, or contact the DOL or your state labor department.",
        "question": "What if a company treats me like an employee but pays me as a contractor?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Not from the business. You plan and fund your own time off.",
        "question": "Do 1099 therapists get paid time off?"
      }
    ],
