    "slug": "administrative-support-therapists-should-expect",
    "title": "What Administrative Support Should Therapists Expect?",
    "primary_question": "What administrative support should an independent therapist expect from a practice or network?",
    "summary": "What real administrative support for therapists looks like, from credentialing to billing to scheduling, and how to tell it apart from vague promises.",
    "body_markdown": "## What does real administrative support include?\n\nSpecific tasks owned by someone other than you. A practical list:\n\n| Task | Supported setting handles it |\n| --- | --- |\n| Credentialing and recredentialing | Yes |\n| Eligibility and benefits checks | Yes |\n| Authorizations and extensions | Yes |\n| Claims submission and follow-up | Yes |\n| Denials and appeals | Yes |\n| Scheduling system and reminders | Yes |\n| Intake and matching | Yes |\n| Client billing questions | Yes |\n| Technology, including the EHR | Yes |\n| A reachable person when something breaks | Yes |\n\nDocumentation, clinical decisions, and your license stay yours anywhere.\n\n## How do you tell real support from a slogan?\n\nAsk specific questions and listen for specific answers:\n\n- \"Who verifies eligibility, and when?\"\n- \"If a claim is denied, who fixes it, and does it affect my pay?\"\n- \"Who handles authorizations and extensions?\"\n- \"Who do I contact with a problem, and how fast do they usually answer?\"\n- \"What does the EHR do for documentation?\"\n\n\"We've got you covered\" isn't an answer. \"Our billing team handles all denials, and you're paid either way\" is.\n\n## What's reasonable to expect on response time?\n\nFor routine questions, the same business day is a reasonable expectation in a supported setting. For urgent operational issues, like a client who can't log in to a session, faster. Ask what's typical, not just what's promised.\n\n## What support shouldn't you expect?\n\n- Clinical supervision, if you're fully licensed and working as a contractor, unless it's offered.\n- Malpractice coverage, in most contractor arrangements.\n- Someone to do your notes.\n\n## How ValorWell handles this\n\nValorWell takes on the business infrastructure: credentialing, authorizations, billing, claims submission, payer follow-up, reimbursement administration, denials, scheduling and telehealth technology, intake, and referrals. The admin team generally responds within a few hours, if not immediately.\n\nIts EHR was built with clinician input and includes AI-assisted documentation. When something isn't working, ValorWell's first move is to adjust its systems and add support.\n\n## Important limits\n\n- **ValorWell doesn't offer formal clinical consultation** as a service, and doesn't provide malpractice coverage.\n- **Documentation remains yours.** Clean claims depend on complete notes.\n- **Support doesn't eliminate every admin task.** You'll still manage your availability and complete your notes.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "Often billing, scheduling, and intake. Credentialing and denial handling vary, so ask.",
        "question": "What admin support do group practices usually provide?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "It depends on the arrangement. Networks that handle billing usually provide significant admin support; pure referral platforms may not.",
        "question": "Should a contractor get admin support?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Vague answers about who handles denials, or pay that depends on claims being collected.",
        "question": "What's a red flag about admin support?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Same-day for routine questions is a reasonable expectation. Ask current clinicians how it works in practice.",
        "question": "How fast should an admin team respond?"
      }
    ],
