    "slug": "precertification-change",
    "title": "What Changed When VA Ended Community Care Precertification",
    "primary_question": "What changed when VA ended Community Care precertification?",
    "summary": "VA stopped requiring precertification for authorized Community Care on September 8, 2026. Authorization and the Request for Service process still apply.",
    "body_markdown": "## What changed?\n\nVA dropped one extra step. VA's [precertification page](https://department.va.gov/vha/community-care/care-coordination/precertification-requirements/) says: effective September 8, 2026, VA no longer requires precertification before providing authorized care for Veterans. The VA Precertification Portal (VAPP) is offline.\n\n## What was precertification?\n\nIt was a notice step VA added for community providers in 2026. For services on a VA list of billing codes, providers had to log in to the precertification portal and get a precertification ID before the date of service, even though the Veteran already had an authorization. VA had announced that claims without a precertification ID would start being denied in September 2026. VA ended the requirement before that happened.\n\n## What stayed the same?\n\nAlmost everything else. VA's page tells providers to:\n\n- **Keep delivering care as authorized** under the Veteran's referral and Standardized Episode of Care (SEOC).\n- **Use the Request for Service (RFS) process** for care the authorization or SEOC doesn't cover.\n- **Expect no change to claims or payment.**\n\nSo the authorization still controls what VA pays for. Precertification was an extra check on top of it. Removing it didn't widen what's covered.\n\n## What does this mean for Veterans?\n\nMostly fewer delays. If your provider was waiting on a precertification ID before a procedure or test that's already in your authorization, they don't need it anymore.\n\nWhat you still need to watch:\n\n- Your authorization letter and its end date. See [Your Authorization Letter](/resources/va-community-care/authorization-letter).\n- New services your provider wants to add. Those still need an RFS. See [Why Your Authorization Doesn't Cover Everything](/resources/va-community-care/services-outside-authorization).\n\n## What does this mean for providers?\n\n- Stop sending precertification requests. The portal is offline.\n- Check the referral and SEOC before each service, as before.\n- Send an RFS, VA Form 10-10172, for additional visits, new specialties, or services outside the SEOC. See [How Community Providers Request More Care](/resources/va-community-care/requests-for-service).\n- For questions, VA says to contact your third-party administrator: Optum for Regions 1 to 3, or TriWest for Regions 4 and 5.\n\n## A common misunderstanding\n\n\"Precertification ended\" doesn't mean \"authorization ended.\" A provider still can't go beyond the referral and SEOC and expect VA to pay. If you hear otherwise from a provider's office, point them to VA's precertification page and the RFS process.",
    "faq": [
      {
        "answer": "No. VA ended the requirement on September 8, 2026, and the precertification portal is offline.",
        "question": "Does VA still require precertification for Community Care?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Yes. Anything outside your authorization or SEOC still needs a Request for Service.",
        "question": "Does my provider still need VA approval for new services?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "VA says ending precertification has no impact on claims processing or payment.",
        "question": "Will claims be denied for missing a precertification ID?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "VA says to contact the third-party administrator: Optum for Regions 1 to 3, or TriWest for Regions 4 and 5.",
        "question": "Who should providers contact with questions?"
      }
    ],
